{} "Date"] [:td {} "29 – 31 July 1976"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/554"} "Bassin olympique, Île Notre Dame, Montréal, Québec"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255454"} "19 from 19 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10046"} "Géza Csapó"] " had many canoeing accolades, among which were victories in the K-1 1000 at the World Championships in 1973 and 1974 and an Olympic bronze medal from 1972, all of which made him a favorite to win gold in the event at the 1976 Summer Games. The most recent World Championships, however, had ended in a tie between Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10197"} "Oreste Perri"] " and Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10522"} "Grzegorz Śledziewski"] ", the latter of whom was also the 1971 champion and the 1973 and 1974 runner-up. The Soviet Union’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11013"} "Oleksandr Shaparenko"] ", meanwhile, was the defending Olympic champion and 1968 runner-up, while East Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9824"} "Rüdiger Helm"] " was the most recent World bronze medalist. In the opening round, Perri, Helm, and Shaparenko won their heats, while Csapó and Śledziewski were runners-up to Perri and Shaparenko respectively. The tables turned in the semi-finals, when Helm and Shaparenko were second to Śledziewski and Csapó’s Olympic record-setting ties respectively, with Perri capturing the third heat. The final developed into a race to the finish between Csapó and Helm, the latter of whom edged out the former by 0.64 seconds, leaving the Hungarian with silver. Bronze was taken by " [:a {:href "/athletes/10581"} "Vasile Dîba"] " of Romania, 1974 and 1975 World Champion and runner-up respectively in the K-1 500, who also won the K-1 500 in Montreal. Helm, meanwhile, also earned bronze medals in the K-1 500 and K-4 1000 at these Games."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead 